The most fundamental part of a CPU is the Instruction Set Architecture (ISA). When starting our project, aside from bit width and instruction width, 
this was the first part that needed to be defined. Since I am a CE noob, I decided to pick something I was already familiar with, so I decided to
have my ISA be inspired by the format of MIPS16. This is the base ISA:

- Instruction Width: 16 bits
- Data Width: 8 bits
- Address width: 12 bits

R-Type:  [ op: 4 bits | Rd: 4 bits | Rx: 4 bits | funct: 4 bits]
I1-Type: [ op: 4 bits | Rd: 4 bits |         Imm: 8 bits       ]
I2-Type: [ op: 4 bits |              Imm: 12 bits              ]
J-Type:  [ op: 4 bits |              Imm: 12 bits              ]

This architecture was modified from MIPS16's op-code width of 3 bits, register width of 3 bits, and funct width of 4 bits. The register bit-width
is the biggest difference between ours and MIPS: since MIPS has 3 bits for register indexing, they can fit in 3 registers per instruction. This 
allows you to have two source registers and a completely separate destination register for commands like "add a, b, c". I neglected to do this 
for a few reasons: 

Through careful thought and consideration, I decided that the added complexity of  creating an architecture that uses three registers at a time 
is not worth the added convenience of simpler programs. I decide, possibly foolishly, that simply having pseudo instructions that do that for you 
are totally fine. In addition, since we are using CPLD's as the hardware for the CPU, execution speed is not an issue, and more instructions are 
likely to be doable, as I am estimating this could be a 5Mhz CPU in the best case, which is plenty fast. 

Second, having 3 bits register indexing means we have only have 8 registers. While this may be fine, I would prefer to have more registers if 
game logic execution is the primary job of this CPU. Having a register bit-wdth of 4 ups that to 16 registers, which gives programmers flexibility
where I think it matters. In addtion, this allows the VLSI part of our project to be more difficult, givng us more credibility. Of course, you may 
ask why trade difficulty in one place for another. The asnwer is that the VLSI cred is easier to implement and simultaneosly cooler (to me) than 
architecture cred. Dave from TI most likley won't care that it can do triple register instructions anyway. 

Using four bits for register indexing allows us 16 registers. I plan to use a lot of these as "temp" registers. The last two instruction types, 
I2 and J, don't use a register at all. Since we have a address width of 12 bits, we don't have any space for registers. These two instructions 
will, by default, use one of the 16 registers, which will be designated as a temp register. Speaking of, let me show you how I plan to lay out the 
