The way I am planning on doing 
this is by using temp registers. The temp registers are special registers that the CPU uses for memory access. If a programmer wanted to 
read from memory, they would execute a read command to an address, then either store that data in a register for later, or use the temp 
register when making calculations. The I2 instructions will move data to these registers, $T0 and $T1, depening on the command. The 
values will be overwritten when the command is run, but otherwise are perfectly good registers.

Another thing to note is the LOAD and STORE instructions inside I1 instructions. You may notice they use an 8-bit IMM width to hold the 
memory address they want to store in, and that the addressable memory in this ISA is 12-bit. Firstly, I'd just like to say you're very
observent ;). But yes, this is correct behavior. The immediate address is limited, so the top 256 Bytes are informally designated as
the "Zero Page". This chunk of bytes are easier to get to thanks to this function. Instead of the two lines of:

  1 LDAT0 0x00F
  2 MOV $R0, $T0

We simply have:

  1 LOAD $R0, 0x00F

While this isn't a huge performance gain, it definetly reduces the amount of code you need for writes and reads, and when you're writing
a game loop that has 10-20 saves, the brevity is much appreciated. Now that you are thinking about the address specification, you might
